Avialable on request
Abstract 

This report presents an investment recommendation for Tesla, Inc. (NASDAQ: TSLA), the world's most valuable automotive company, valued as of 30 July 2026. Two complementary methodologies are applied, a discounted cash flow (DCF) valuation using the free cash flow to the firm approach, which reveals the intrinsic value of the equity, and a relative valuation through trading multiples, which provides a market-anchored valuation. The report analyses five years of historical financial data (fiscal years 2021 to 2025) and forecasts the following ten years at segment level, identifying external and internal value drivers for each of Tesla's three businesses: automotive, energy generation and storage, and services. 

The DCF, discounting segment-built free cash flows at a WACC of 7.83 per cent, produces an equity value of $616.2 billion and an implied share price of $184.00, a 40.53 per cent discount to the market price of $309.22. A sensitivity analysis shows the estimate is robust between $135 and $300 across wide ranges of WACC and terminal growth, and a scenario analysis presents a bearish case ($61) and a bullish case ($417). The relative valuation, applying the peer median EV/EBITDA multiple of 12x drawn from BYD, Toyota, General Motors and Ford, independently produces $176 per share, corroborating the DCF. A deliberately constructed market-implied scenario demonstrates that even near-flawless execution of Tesla's autonomy and robotics ambitions supports only approximately $417 per share. 

The report concludes that the findings of the DCF approach and the relative valuation approach jointly indicate an investment recommendation of UNDERWIEGHT. 
